// Coch-y-Bonddu: an old Welsh beetle imitation: gold tag, peacock herl body, coch-y-bonddu hackle.

id coch-y-bonddu
name "Coch-y-Bonddu"
aliases "Coch-y-Bondhu" "Shorn Fly" "Marlow Buzz" "Bracken Clock"
type dry
imitates terrestrial
naturals beetle
difficulty beginner
species trout grayling sea-trout
season may june july august
origin region="Wales"
description "A flat gold tag, a plump body of bronze peacock herl ribbed with gold wire, and a red-and-black coch-y-bonddu hackle."
history """
    A traditional Welsh fly imitating a small beetle, which Mary Orvis Marbury says is
    sometimes called the bracken clock; Rusty Dunn names it as the garden chafer. Charles
    Bowlker's Art of Angling (1792 edition) has it as the Shorn Fly: a red cock's hackle
    "with a black list up the middle" and a peacock herl body. The 1854 edition adds that it
    is much used in Wales and better known there as Coch-a-bonddu, "red with a black body",
    after the hackle. Fred Klein traces peacock soft hackles back to Dennys's Secrets of
    Angling (1613); that is unverified.
    """
fishing """
    Bowlker's 1854 edition has it at its best in June but useful all season, and says many
    anglers always have one on as a dropper. Fish it dry in summer, when beetles are blown
    onto the water on windy days; tied wet with a soft hen hackle it is a good general
    wet fly. Courtney Williams, as quoted by Dunn: "Wet or dry, and at any time of the
    season, it is always a good pattern to try on a strange river."
    """
sources {
    "Bowlker: The Art of Angling (1792)" url="https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-art-of-angling-or-_bowlker-charles_1792"
    "Bowlker's Art of Angling, new edition (1854)" url="https://archive.org/details/bowlkersartofang00bowlrich"
    "SWTU: Coch-y-Bonddu (Rusty Dunn)" url="https://www.swtu.org/2020/03/05/coch-y-bonddu/"
    "Midcurrent: A Fly Fishing Chronicle: The Coch-y-Bonddu (Fred Klein)" url="https://midcurrent.com/flies/a-fly-fishing-chronicle-the-coch-y-bonddu/"
    "Fish4Flies: Coch-y-Bondhu" url="https://www.fish4flies.co.uk/Dry/Terrestrials/Coch-y-bondhu"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://www.fish4flies.com/img/flies/Large/800-800.jpg" page="https://www.fish4flies.co.uk/Dry/Terrestrials/Coch-y-bondhu" license="All rights reserved; reference only" flip=#true note="Side view, dry, gold tag"
    "https://www.swtu.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/coch-y-bonddu.jpg" page="https://www.swtu.org/2020/03/05/coch-y-bonddu/" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view, wet version"
}

// Assumption: a down-eye dry hook; the traditional British choice is an up-eye Captain Hamilton.
hook dry-standard size=12
thread black size="8/0"

materials {
    tag flat-tinsel color=gold
    rib gold-wire
    body peacock-herl strands=4 label="Bronze peacock herl"
    hackle cock-hackle color=coch fibre=1.5 label="Coch-y-bonddu (or furnace) cock hackle"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.9
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    tie-in tag
    wrap-thread to=0.08
    wind tag to=0.06 turns=3 note="Three or four touching turns at the start of the bend."
    tie-off tag
    tie-in rib
    tie-in body note="Twist the herls into a rope for strength."
    wrap-thread to=0.72
    wind body to=0.72 turns=9 note="Touching turns, a plump acorn-shaped body."
    tie-off body
    wind rib to=0.72 turns=5 counter=#true note="Through the herl."
    tie-off rib
    tie-in hackle
    wrap-thread to=0.9
    wind hackle to=0.88 turns=4
    tie-off hackle
    whip-finish
}
